## 1. INTRODUCTION
Polymer solar cells (PSCs) are promising candidates as wearable power sources because of their lightweight and flexibility. For the development of reliable wearable electronics, high power conversion efficiency (PCE) and mechanical robustness of PSCs are important prerequisites, and recent developments of efficient polymer donor $(P_{\mathsf{D}})$ and small molecule acceptor (SMA) materials have achieved PCEs over $18-19\%$ .[1] Meanwhile, the stretchability of these high-performance PSCs is typically low (i.e., crack-onset strain $(C O S)\ <\ 5\%)$ and the resulting thin films are generally brittle. The low mechanical robustness of these high-performance systems can be attributed to the disconnected crystallites of the photoactive materials and their sharp boundaries, as the PD:SMA materials typically include rigid chemical structures with high crystallinity.[2] Consequently, mechanical stresses are concentrated on the sharp crystal-to-crystal interfaces of the active materials and the cracks can easily propagate through the interfaces when the films are stretched.[3] Therefore, improving the stretchability of PSCs with high PCEs is a critical step toward the application of PSCs in wearable devices.
