To this end, different research groups have proposed various molecular design strategies to improve the stretchability of active materials.[4] One of the key strategies to reinforce the mechanical robustness of PSCs is incorporating elastomers (i.e. polydimethylsiloxane (PDMS) and styrene-ethylene-butylene-styrene (SEBS)) into the PD:SMA blend films.[5] These elastomers can increase the stretchability of the blend film due to their higher stretchability $(>100-200\%)$ compared to rigid conjugated materials. For example, Chen e al emonstrated that the addition of poly(dimethylsiloxane-comethyl phenethylsiloxane) elastomer in all-polymer blends effectively increases the stretchability of blend films.[6] In addition, Peng e al.introduced SEBS elastomers as the third component in high-performance PD:SMA systems, and successfully improved the PCE and stretchability of the PSCs.[3a] However, the addition of elastomers often induces the strong phase separation of elastomer domains from the active domains and deteriorates the charge generation and transport properties of the PSCs.[3a,7] For instance, introducing 30 wt% of the SEBS elastomers into the PM6:N3 active system drastically increases the domain size of blends from 17 to 242 nm and reduces the PCE of the PSCs
