from 15.4 to $11.3\%$ .[3a] Therefore, molecular design for preventing strong phase separation between the elastomers and conjugated polymers is important to increase the stretchability of the blend films without compromising the PCEs of the PSCs.
Block copolymerization of the elastomers and conjugated polymers can be a promising strategy to realize highly efficient PSCs with enhanced stretchability. Highly stretchable elastomer blocks can function as molecular springs that bridge the hard conjugated blocks, significantly increasing the stretchability of the resulting block copolymer (BCP). In addition, the macrophase separation between the elastomer and active components can be effectively suppressed in ${\mathsf{B C P S}},$ because of the covalent bond between the elastomer and conjugated polymer blocks. For instance, the feasibility of the elastomer-conjugated polymer BCP has been successfully demonstrated in the organic field effect transistor.[8] Muller e al.developed BCPs consisting of poly(3-hexylthiophene) (P3HT) and polyethylene (PE) (P3HT- $b$ -PE, P3HT:PE $\mathbf{\tau}=\mathbf{\tau}$ 35:65 in weight ratio), and demonstrated high stretchability and electrical properties of the synthesized BCP; the P3HT-b-PE-based stretchable OFET devices exhibited significantly higher stretchability
