While the stretchabilities of the PM6- $b$ -PDMS:L8-BO blend films are superior to that of the PM6:L8-BO film and PM6:L8-BO:PDMS ternary film, we also observed that the stretchability of the PM6- $b$ -PDMS:L8-BO blend films strongly depends on the PDMS block length in PM6- $b$ -PDMS $\rho_{\mathsf{D}}\mathsf{s}$ . For example, the COS values significantly increase from $4.30\%$ for PM6-b-PDMS $5\mathsf{k}$ :L8-BO film, $8.13\%$ for PM6- $b$ -PDMS $12\mathsf{k}$ :L8-BO film to $18.14\%$ for PM6- $b$ -PDMS $_{19\mathrm{k}}$ :L8-BO blend film (Figure 3b). This is attributed to the enhanced stretchability of PDMS block domains with increasing $M_{\mathfrak{n}}$ of the PDMS block. In particular, we expect that the PDMS chains of the PM6- $\cdot b$ -PDMS19k become well-entangled as this molecular weight is close to the critical molecular weight, twice of entanglement molecular weight $(M_{\mathrm{e}}\sim10\ k g\ m o l^{-1}$ ).[11a,12c,21] The entangled polymer networks can effectively dissipate the external mechanical stress, and, thus, significantly increase the stretchability of the polymers.[22] Collectively, the PM6- $b.$ -PDMS19k:L8-BO blend films successfully achieved both high PCE $(\sim18\%)$ and stretchability $(\mathsf{C O S}=18.1\%)$ among the blend films.
