6a – 6b).[27] The detailed fabrication procedures and conditions for the IS-PSC are described in Experimental Section.
The IS-PSCs based on the PM6:L8-BO and PM6- $b$ -PDMS:L8-BO blend films showed the similar initial PCEs ( $11.2\ -\ 12.0\%)$ , but they showed distinct differences in PCE changes during the strain (Figure 6c – 6d). Without the elastomeric PDMS block, the PM6:L8-BO-based IS-PSC showed a sharp decrease in the PCE when the applied strain is larger than $10\%$ . In contrast, the PM6- $b$ -PDMS:L8-BO-based IS-PSCs retained the initial PCEs at a higher strain value, where the enhanced device stretchability of the BCP-based IS-PSCs is more distinct with the longer PDMS block. For example, the strains at $\mathsf{P C E}_{80\%}$ (the strain value maintaining $80\%$ of the initial PCE) of the PM6:L8-BO-, PM6-bPDMS5k:L8-BO-, PM6- $b$ -PDMS12k:L8-BO-, and PM6- $b_{}$ -PDMS $_{19\mathrm{k}}$ :L8-BO-based IS-PSCs were 12.3, 17.1, 25.7, and $36.5\%,$ respectively. In contrast to the performance of IS-PSCs using BCP $\bar{P}_{\mathsf{D}},$ the IS-PSCs based on the PM6:L8-BO:PDMS $12\mathrm{k}$ ternary blend film showed low PCE $(2.58\%)$ and mechanical stretchability (strain at $\mathsf{P C E}_{80\%}=3.9\%)$ .
