## 3. CONCLUSIONS
In summary, we achieved highly efficient $(\mathsf{P C E}\sim18\%)$ and mechanically robust $(C O S\sim18\%)$ PSCs by developing PM6-block-poly(dimethylsiloxane) (PM6- $b$ -PDMSx, $\chi=$ 5, 12, and 19k) block copolymer donors. The covalent linking of PM6 and PDMS blocks effectively suppressed the phase separation between the active components and elastomers, while preserving the excellent electrical properties of PM6 blocks. At the same time, the PDMS blocks in PM6-b-PDMS BCPs served as molecular springs that bridge crystalline PM6 domains and significantly increased the stretchability of blend thin films. As a result, the PM6- $b$ -PDMSx:L8-BO blend achieved excellent PSC PCE $(\sim18\%)$ and thin-film stretchability $(\mathsf{C O S}>10\%)$ , which are significantly higher than that of PM6:L8- BO blend $(\mathsf{C O S}=2.3\%)$ ). We demonstrated that the stretchability of the PM6- $b_{}$ -PDMS:L8- BO strongly depends on the PDMS block length in the PM6- $b$ -PDMS $\bar{P_{\mathsf{D}}}\mathsf{S},$ and the BCP with the longest PDMS block (PM6- $b\cdot$ -PDMS19k) affored the blend films with the highest stretchability $(\mathsf{C O S}=18.1\%)$ ). In contrast, the ternary blend films of PM6:L8-BO:PDMS12k exhibit poor performance $(\mathsf{P C E}\ \sim\ 4\%$ and $\mathsf{C O S}~=~1.1\%$ due to the strong phase
This article is protected by copyright. All rights reserved.
separation of PDMS and PM6 domains. We further examined the performance of PM6- $b$ - PDMS19k:L8-BO-based blend in IS-PSCs and demonstrated both high PCE $\sim$ $11.3\%$ and excellent stretchability of $36\%$ strain at $\mathsf{P C E}_{80\%}$ (maintaining $80\%$ of initial PCE). This represents one of the best IS-PSC performances reported to date, demonstrating the great potential of designing block copolymer donors for their applications in wearable electronics.
